Relationships Between Bitcoin Prices and Regulation-Related News

This project analyzes the relationship between Bitcoin price movements and regulation-related news coverage to evaluate whether regulatory events and media attention have a measurable impact on Bitcoin’s daily price changes.

Rather than analyzing multiple cryptocurrencies, this study focuses exclusively on Bitcoin, allowing for clearer insights using historical price data and regulation-focused news articles.

Research Questions

Does regulation-related news correlate with daily percentage changes in Bitcoin price?

Does the volume of regulation-related news articles influence Bitcoin price movement?

Can machine learning models predict whether Bitcoin’s price will increase or decrease based on news-derived features?

Methodology
Data Collection

Bitcoin historical price data

Regulation-related news articles collected using NewsAPI

Data Processing

Cleaning and aligning price data with daily news article counts

Feature engineering on:

Daily percent price change

News article volume

Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA)

Statistical summaries of Bitcoin price movements

Comparison of price behavior on days with and without regulation-related news

Visualization of trends between news volume and volatility

Machine Learning Models

Logistic Regression – classify price movement (up vs. down)

Linear Regression – predict percent price change

Random Forest Regression – model non-linear relationships

Key Findings

Regulation-related news demonstrates limited direct predictive power over Bitcoin’s daily price changes.

Higher news volume does not consistently correspond to stronger positive or negative price movements.

Machine learning models struggled to outperform baseline assumptions, highlighting Bitcoin’s high volatility and the influence of external market factors beyond news coverage alone.

Tech Stack

Python

pandas, NumPy

matplotlib, seaborn

scikit-learn

NewsAPI

Jupyter Notebook

